    "text": "A complex number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></math> is said to be unimodular if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. Let,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are complex numbers such that <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mo>-</mo> <mn>2z</mn><msub><mtext></mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>2</mn> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>z</mtext> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <msub> <mrow> <mover accent=\"true\"> <mtext>z</mtext> <mo>¯</mo> </mover> </mrow> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> is unimodular and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is not unimodular, then the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> lies on a&nbsp;",
